{{ macros.section(2, "Concepts", id="concepts") }}

Beyond the [compiler]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) syntax and the [banana block]({{_}}/cmk/banana), a small set of runtime concepts recur across CMK-Lang.  This page is a landing outline that collects them; each has its fuller treatment on the page it links to.

These build on each other.  An **ambient** is the top idea, a named place a block can *move*, and it carries the whole mobility algebra (`in` / `out` / `open` / `|` / `+`).  A **machine** is an ambient a block can *run in*; a **host** runs it on the local machine and a **container** runs it in docker.  A **namespace** is an ambient a block can *dissolve into*, and a **module** (a runnable namespace) is one a block can also *exec in*: `(| code |) in M` runs the code against the module's own targets, the way `(| .. |) in <container>` runs it in docker.  Machine, namespace, and module are kinds of ambient that wire up different parts of the same algebra.  **Goals**, finally, are the unit of work the runtime actually resolves.

{{ macros.img_link("concepts.png", mkdocs, "90%", class=" ") }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Ambients", id="ambients") }}

**Ambients** are like a kind of folder / membrane that makes the idea of *inside vs outside* concrete.  If you like the folder *(=namespace)* analogy, you can also imagine little sticky-notes inside *(=data)*, and event throw in some ants *(=agents)* crawling around.  Notice that the folders themselves are as mobile as the notes and the ants.  Neat model, huh?


So, ambients are an abstraction for *a block of mobile computation*.  Since [banana-brackets](#) and the other abstractions obey an [ambient-algebra](#ambient-algebra), they can **move between execution contexts**.

 In CMK one rarely reaches for ambients *directly*, but the algebra itself sits beneath many other concepts on this page.  For example [namespaces](#namespaces), [machines](#machines), and their subkinds all specialize it.

{{ macros.img_link("ambients.png", mkdocs, "85%", class=" ") }}

{% include "includes/ambient-algebra.md" %}

{{ macros.section(5, "Ambient Inwards", id="ambient-inwards") }}

`in` names a **destination**: it relocates a block into an ambient and runs it there.  The destination may be any [machine](#machines) -- a host interpreter, a [container](#containers), or a service -- so the *same* block runs unchanged wherever you send it.  Its dual is [`out`](#ambient-outwards).

{% set in_host %}
# a host interpreter: runs locally, no docker
(| echo "hi from $(hostname)" |) in host.native.bash
{% endset %}
{% set in_container %}
# the same block, relocated into docker: declare the
# image once, then send the block in
container ubuntu(img=ubuntu:22.04)(| |)
(| echo "hi from $(hostname)" |) in ubuntu
{% endset %}
{{ site.inline_mirror('mirror_ambient_in', in_host, in_container,
    label_left='Host', type_left='cmk', lang_left='cmk',
    label_right='Container', default='left') }}

{{ macros.section(5, "Ambient Open", id="ambient-open") }}

`open` names a **boundary to dissolve**.  Where [`in`](#ambient-inwards) runs a block *inside* an ambient, `open` brings an ambient's contents *out* into the caller: it merges a module or [namespace](#namespaces)'s definitions into the current scope rather than relocating a block into it.  A [machine](#machines) is what you `in`; a namespace is what you `open`.

It is the [`Openable`](#ambient-protocol) protocol: each target kind supplies its own opener, so the one word dissolves a plugin, a file, or the prelude, and a star form dissolves an anonymous block in place:

{% set open_mk %}
# a bare name -> include a plugin
$(call include.plugins, strict=0 some.plugin.cmk)

# a /-path -> import a file, members merged flat
$(call import.module, file=./lib.cmk flat=1)

# the prelude -> bind the declaration keywords bare
$(call cmk.__open__)

# no call form: the star's body is spliced flat, in place
helper:; @echo hi
{% endset %}
{% set open_cmk %}
open some.plugin          # a bare name -> include a plugin
open ./lib.cmk            # a /-path    -> import a file
open cmk                  # the prelude -> bind the keywords bare
*(| helper:; @echo hi |)  # star form   -> dissolve a block in place
{% endset %}
{{ site.inline_mirror('mirror_ambient_open', open_mk, open_cmk, default='cmk') }}

{{ macros.section(5, "Ambient Outwards", id="ambient-outwards") }}

`out` is the dual of [`in`](#ambient-inwards): it names no destination, it moves the block *one level out*, to whatever ambient encloses it.  "Encloses" is a runtime fact -- resolved **dynamically** against the call chain rather than the lexical structure -- so where the block lands depends on where the recipe is called from:

{% set out_recipe %}
# top-level recipe: the enclosing ambient is the host,
# so `out` escapes to host.local and runs there (default)
(| echo "on $(hostname)" |) out
{% endset %}
{% set out_container %}
# inside a container: `out` escapes to the host daemon
# via the mounted docker socket (the "exit capability")
(| echo "on $(hostname)" |) out
{% endset %}
{{ site.inline_mirror('mirror_ambient_out', out_recipe, out_container,
    label_left='From a recipe', type_left='cmk', lang_left='cmk',
    label_right='From a container', default='left') }}

Three cases fall out: from a **top-level recipe** the enclosing ambient is the host; from a **named ambient** `out` navigates into that parent; and from **inside `host.local`** there is nothing further out, so `out` raises a named fault, `OutwardsUndefined: this is the top`.

{{ macros.section(4, "Ambient Protocol", id="ambient-protocol", protocols=['__ambients__', 'Openable']) }}

Besides the algebra, there's the [ambient protocol](#protocols): the ambient superkind conforms to `__ambients__` and `Openable`.

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['Banana Blocks: As Ambient', '@/cmk/banana/#banana-ambient'],
    'the `demos/cmk/ambients.cmk` (recipe algebra) and `demos/cmk/ambients-open.cmk` (module `open`) demos',
], mkdocs) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Machines", id="machines", protocols=['Runnable']) }}

A **machine** is an ambient a block can *run in*: any *run context* a block can execute in, the kind of ambient you `in`.  Every runnable block runs through some machine's `.run` hook.  Two subkinds fix *where* that hook runs: a **host** on the local machine, a **container** in docker.  Both derive the abstract base [`cmk.machine`]({{_}}/cmk/banana/#banana-declare), so every runnable ambient is a host or a container.

{{ macros.img_link("concepts-machine.png", mkdocs, "70%", class=" ") }}

*Where* it runs (the is-a tree above) is separate from its *capabilities* (structural interfaces, via [`cmk.protocol`](#protocols)): a machine conforms to [`Runnable`](#protocols) (the `in`-dual: it executes a block), [`Callable`](#protocols), and [`Ambient`](#protocols) (the mobility-tree membership a [namespace](#namespaces) also has).  A **code-object** (an inline `code` / `polyglot` block) is *content*, not a machine: it sits off the machine axis, but still carries capabilities -- it is [`Materializable`](#protocols) (its body can be handed to a tool as a file) and a [`Templatable`](#protocols) (it renders, filling holes).  It *may be* **bound** to a machine, gaining runnability by delegation, or left **unbound** -- materializable, templatable source, but not directly runnable.  So "polyglot is-a machine" is wrong: a code-object *has-a* binding, and runs only once hydrated by one.

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['Banana Blocks: As Machine', '@/cmk/banana/#banana-machine'],
], mkdocs) }}

{{ macros.section(4, "Host", id="host", kind="Machine") }}

A **host** *is a machine*: one that runs on the local machine (no docker).  The always-present singleton **`host.local`** is the default shell, the canonical `out` target, and the home for host capabilities, and the native interpreters (**`host.native.*`**, one per shell) back the bare {{ cmkmath.imath("(|..|) #in #bash") }} / {{ cmkmath.imath("#in #python") }} forms.

{{ macros.section(4, "Containers", id="containers", kind="Machine") }}

A **container** *is a machine*: the twin of `host`, one whose `.run` is pinned to a docker container instead of the local machine.  Declared inline ({{ cmkmath.imath("#container @NAME (|..|)") }}) or imported from a compose service, it is the bridge between CMK-Lang blocks and the wider container-dispatch machinery.

{{ macros.img_link("concepts-container.png", mkdocs, "55%", class=" ") }}

A banana handed to the built-in **`container`** constructor names a runnable image; the ambient operator [`in`](#ambient-inwards) then runs any block *inside* it.  You declare the container from an image and send a block in; the block is materialized and executed in the image, so a Python body runs under a stock image with no Dockerfile in sight.

{% set container_declare %}
# name a runnable image (entrypoint optional)
container box(img=python:3.12-slim entrypoint=python3)(| |)
{% endset %}
{% set container_use %}
# send any block in; it runs inside the image
(| print("6 * 7 =", 6 * 7) |) in box
{% endset %}
{{ site.inline_mirror('mirror_container', container_declare, container_use,
    label_left='Declare', type_left='cmk', lang_left='cmk',
    label_right='Send a block in', default='left') }}

Being a [machine](#machines), `container` plugs into the same run-side [ambient algebra](#ambient-algebra) as the host runners.  It is a prelude keyword, bare after opening the prelude, `cmk.container` otherwise.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-in.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['Container Dispatch', '@/container-dispatch'],
    ['Import Services from Compose', '@/bridge'],
], mkdocs) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Namespaces", id="namespaces", protocols=['Ambient', 'Openable']) }}

A **namespace** is the *other* kind of ambient: one a block **dissolves into** rather than runs in.  [`open`](#ambient-open) merges its definitions into the current scope, and a {{ cmkmath.imath("#namespace @NAME [|..|]") }} (or an imported **module**) qualifies its own heads under a fully-qualified prefix.  Where a machine wires the `in` side of the algebra, a namespace wires `open`; its [dual](#ambients) -- running a block with that namespace in scope -- is the natural completion.

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['Modules', '@/cmk/modules', 'the module system built on namespaces (`import` / `open` / packaging)'],
    ['Banana Blocks: As Ambient', '@/cmk/banana/#banana-ambient'],
], mkdocs) }}

