{{ macros.section(3, "Protocols", id="protocols") }}

{{ macros.img_link("concepts-protocol.png", mkdocs, "80%", class=" ") }}

*Where* a kind sits in the is-a tree is separate from *what capabilities* it exposes.  A **protocol** is CMK's take on Python's data model: an interface for a dunder (or member family), created by `cmk.protocol` and checked **structurally** -- conformance is by *having* the members (`.provided_by`, Python's `__subclasshook__`), no registration needed.  `cmk.protocol` is itself a metaclass, so protocols are first-class objects that publish `.abstract` / `.__mro__`, and a kind mixes several in via `bases=` (`cmk.Fragment(bases=Callable, Materializable)`).

Each protocol names a required member; the **body** sets the tier.  A docstring-only body is **structural** -- a contract with no default, conformance incidental.  A body that assigns a real member is **concretized** -- it ships a default implementation, so it has implementors and stamps that default onto conformers via `bases=`.

| Protocol | Requires | Tier | Capability |
|---|---|---|---|
| [`Documented`](#documented-protocols) | `__doc__` | Structural | Documentation string |
| [`Named`](#named-protocols) | `__name__` | Structural | Qualified identity |
| [`Registry`](#registry-protocols) | `.has` / `.require` | Structural | Queryable named collection (`__plugins__` / `__modules__` / `__ambients__` / `__goals__`) |
| [`Directory`](#directory-protocols) | `__all__` / `__dir__` | Structural | Reflection: export manifest + live member listing |
| [`Callable`](#callable-protocols) | `__call__` | Concretized | Bare invocation |
| [`Loggable`](#loggable-protocols) | `.log` | Concretized | Name-bound logger (built on `Named`) |
| [`Runnable`](#runnable-protocols) | `.run` | Concretized | Execute a block, the `in`-dual |
| [`Program`](#program-protocols) | `__main__` | Concretized | Entrypoint |
| [`Feedable`](#feedable-protocols) | `feed` | Concretized | How a program file reaches its entrypoint (arg / stdin / flag) |
| [`Openable`](#openable-protocols) | `__open__` | Concretized | The `open` / dissolve hook |
| [`Templatable`](#templatable-protocols) | `__mod__` / `render` | Concretized | Compile-time symbol injection |
| [`Materializable`](#materializable-protocols) | `__blockref__` / `.fd` / `.file` | Concretized | Expose a body as a process-substitution FD or tmpfile |
| [`Ambient`](#ambient-protocols) | `__ambient_parent__` | Concretized | Mobility-tree membership |
| [`Language`](#language-protocols) | `__symbols__` | Concretized | Structural reflection over a fragment's own symbols / blocks / tokens |

So a **machine** conforms to `Runnable` + `Callable` + `Ambient`; a **`dsl`** kind to `Callable` + `Materializable` + `Program`; a **`blockref`** to `Materializable` alone (materialization without a call).  A **`module`** adds `Runnable` -- it *runs* foreign code against its own namespace (`(| code |) in M`) -- plus `Openable`, so `in` reaches host, container, and module through the one `Runnable` contract.  Crucially, a **namespace** also conforms to `Ambient`: a base class could never span both a machine and a namespace, but a **protocol** can, which is exactly why the ambient superkind is a protocol and not a class.

Each protocol carries a collapsed card below; the concept headings above badge the protocols they conform to, linking here.

