{{ macros.section(3, "DSLs", id="dsls", protocols=['Materializable']) }}

A **DSL** is a [`constructor`]({{_}}/cmk/banana/#banana-declare) that creates a *sub-language*: its instances carry a banana body written in a **guest language** -- `jq`, `awk`, `python`, ... -- rather than in `make`.  It is a **metaclass** (`dsl NAME[| .. |]` is `class NAME(bases=cmk.Fragment)[| .. |]`), so an instance like `jqlang addone(| .n + 1 |)` is a **fragment**: a quoted piece of guest code that is both [`Callable`](#protocols) (invoke it bare, feeding stdin to the guest program) and [`Materializable`](#protocols) (reference it as a file), and composes with its siblings into a *closed algebra* -- the result is itself a fragment, re-composable rather than a one-shot string.

{{ macros.img_link("dsl.png", mkdocs, "45%", class=" ") }}

**Where it sits.**  A DSL is on the [protocol](#protocols) axis, not the ambient one: a fragment is `Callable` and `Materializable` but **not** `Runnable` or `Ambient` -- it is not a [machine](#machines) and has no `in` / `out`.  A fragment's `.__call__` *invokes* (hands stdin to a guest interpreter), where a machine's `.run` is a *run-context* (dispatches a whole block).  It is also distinct from a bare [code-object](#machines): both are materializable, but a fragment is callable on its own, while a code-object runs only once bound to a machine.  A language kind may additionally reflect on its own body via the [`Language`](#language-protocols) protocol.

The [DSLs page]({{_}}/cmk/dsls) makes it concrete: the built-in `dsl.jqlang` / `dsl.awklang` kinds, creating your own (like a `python` DSL), `dsl` as a `cmk` prelude member, and the closed composition algebra in action.

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['DSLs', '@/cmk/dsls', 'jqlang / awklang, and `dsl` as a `cmk` prelude member'],
    ['Banana Blocks: Constructors', '@/cmk/banana/#banana-declare', 'the constructor family a DSL is created by'],
    'the `demos/cmk/jqlang.cmk` demo (fragments and their closed composition algebra)',
], mkdocs) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Callforms", id="callforms") }}

A **callform** is CMK's unified call syntax: one grammar for invoking any of the three callable kinds, with optional callform-channels for passing arguments, a stream, and an environment.

{% set callform_collapsed = false %}
{% include "includes/callform-algebra.md" %}

Two points the card leaves implicit are worth drawing out.  The *anchor* (a `this` prefix for a target, a `cmk` prefix for a macro, or none for a receiver) is a **prefix, not a keyword**, so [the dialect]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming) can re-set its value.  And the **`[stream]`** channel accepts more than a literal command: what you pipe in may be a verbatim command, an (interpolating) [triple-quote literal]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#triple-quote-literals), a [block-reference]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#block-references), or several of these nested and composed to any depth.

{{ macros.see_also([
    ['Quick Reference: Call Forms', '@/cmk/quickref/#call-forms', 'the grammar table'],
    ['Banana Blocks: As Callables', '@/cmk/banana/#banana-channels', 'the same callform-channels on blocks'],
    ['Compiler Tutorial: Receiver Form', '@/cmk/compiler/#receiver-form', 'the anchorless form + the declaration-forms grammar it is discovered from'],
], mkdocs) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Handles", id="handles") }}

A **handle** is a *deferred, named call*: a first-class reference to a [callform](#callforms) that is **bound but not run**.  It is the lazy twin of the [`<-` capture operator]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#capture-operator): where a plain `x <- RHS` runs the right-hand side *now* and captures its stdout, the **handle-assignment form** `&name <- RHS` binds the RHS and leaves it **unrun**.  Calling the handle later, `name()`, is what runs it.

The leading `&` reads as a *reference declaration* (C++-style `&`): it marks the bind as *by-reference* rather than *by-value*.  Either way the left-hand name is registered as a [receiver](#callforms), so a handle is callable bare, `name()`, `name(args)`, with no anchor.  The two differ in kind: the eager `<-` captures a *value*, while `&` binds a lazy *deferred call*.

The canonical use is **object-centric dispatch**: an unbound [code-object](#machines) holds source and a [machine](#machines) supplies the runtime, and a handle gives that pairing a name so the call site reads like a method (`bound()`) rather than a `machine/object` path.  The demo below binds a handle to an inline elixir snippet's dispatch with `&bound <- ..`, then calls it as `bound()`:

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/handles.cmk', github=github) }}

**Fluent Fragments.**  This is the [fluent banana]({{_}}/cmk/banana/#banana-fluent) chain -- `foo().bar()`, whose operands fold left-to-right through a per-operator dunder -- but *bound* rather than run.  Because the `&` form binds a *callable* (not a captured string), its RHS may be a **fragment expression**: operands joined by the infix composition operators (`|`, `/`, `+`, and `%` for template [fill]({{_}}/cmk/quickref/#string-algebra)), which fold left-associatively into a single composed call (`&pipeline <- addone|double|wrap`).  It is the same fold either way; where the fluent form runs the chain inline, the handle form names it for later.  Bare `<-` stays unambiguously a shell capture, where `/`, `|`, and `%` are ordinary shell characters; the composition algebra lives only inside `&`.

**Handle Dispatch.**  A handle is a receiver, so besides calling it as `name()` you can dispatch it into a [machine](#machines) with `in`: `&greet in host.native.sh` runs the handle in that machine, and `&out <- greet in host.native.sh` captures the run into a recipe variable.  The same *`&`-selects-cmk-lang* rule applies: a plain `greet in host.native.sh` is an ordinary shell command, and `out <- greet in host.native.sh` an ordinary shell capture, so the `&` is exactly what tells the compiler to read the line as cmk rather than hand it to the shell.  This closes the loop with the string algebra: fill a template, bind the result under a handle, then run that handle, all without ever re-embedding its text.

