{{ macros.section(3, "Kinds", id="kinds") }}

"**Kind**" is the word this page reaches for whenever a construct's *category* matters more than its place in the object model -- a [machine](#machines), a [namespace](#namespaces), a [`dsl`](#dsls), a [code-object](#machines), a [`protocol`](#protocols).  It is worth naming the nuance, because "kind" is deliberately looser than `class` / `metaclass` / `instance` / `type`, and the looseness is load-bearing.

**Why not just say "class".**  CMK's object model is a single uniform tower: every construct -- a protocol, a machine, a fragment, an instance -- is created by the *same* class machinery, and the makers are themselves first-class objects (`cmk.protocol` is a metaclass, yet publishes its own `.abstract` / `.__mro__`).  So `class` / `metaclass` / `instance` / `type` are **positional** terms -- they answer "what is this *relative to* something else."  `jqlang` is a *class* relative to `addone`, an *instance* relative to `dsl`, and a *metaclass* only if you ask what creates *its* members.  There is no absolute stratum to pin the word to.

**What "kind" pins instead.**  A kind is that same node named *without committing to a tower level*: the **constructor genus, plus the [protocol](#protocols)-conformance bundle it stamps via `bases=`**.  Saying "a `dsl` kind conforms to `Callable` + `Materializable`", or "`machine`, `namespace`, and `module` are kinds of ambient", is a claim about *what capabilities the category confers*, not about where it sits in an is-a chain.  That is why the one word covers the factory (`dsl`), the class it creates (`jqlang`), and the family it belongs to (`machine`): each is answering "what *sort* of construct is this," which is orthogonal to the created-by relation.

**It is prose, not a primitive.**  There is no `__kind__` dunder, and nothing in the object model dispatches on "kind"; a construct's category lives implicitly in its `.__metaclass__` plus the [protocol](#protocols) set it satisfies, and "kind" is the gloss over that pair.  The one place the word is reified is narrow -- the [`Openable`](#ambient-open) router carries a `kind=` selector to pick a load-subkind's `.__open__` (`module` includes a plugin, `path` imports a file) -- and that is a *kind of open*, not the general term.  Everywhere else, read "kind" as: the category, agnostic to whether you are looking at the mould or the thing it casts.

